Architecture: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the internal architecture of the .sdd system itself — which
  components exist inside .sdd/, how they relate, and which relationships
  are allowed. This is NOT the project's technical architecture (stack,
  module isolation, layers) — that lives in project/architecture.sdd
  (see Note below — similar name, different scope).

Owns:
  .sdd/

Components:

  PROJECT:
    path: ../PROJECT.sdd
    role: Root entry point / router (see PROJECT.sdd -> Rules R1-R3)

  PROJECT_MODEL:
    path: ../project
    role: Project knowledge model (WHAT/WHY/WHERE)

  ARCHITECTURE:
    path: ../architecture
    role: SDD system architecture definitions (this file)

  CHAINS:
    path: ../chains
    role: Execution chains (chain-type model — feature/bugfix/change, STEP 5)

  SKILLS:
    path: ../skills
    role: Engineering skills (HOW, STEP 6)

  PROMPTS:
    path: ../prompts
    role: Prompt intelligence (inbox, analysis, STEP 7)

  TASKS:
    path: ../tasks/tasks.sdd
    role: Task ENGINE (HOW a task works — lifecycle, creation, decomposition,
      dependency, state, review, failure, recovery, relocation, completion).
      Root-level, because this is .sdd's own system behavior, not project
      knowledge (portability). Concrete task instances ("what does it belong
      to?") live under their own domain (../project/<domain>/tasks/*.sdd),
      not in this component (STEP 8 final, see ../tasks/tasks.sdd -> Note)

  DECISIONS:
    path: ../decisions
    role: Human decisions (decision lifecycle, Rules [D1]-[D12], STEP 9)

  STATE:
    path: ../state
    role: System state


Relationships:

  PROJECT
    -> PROJECT_MODEL

  PROJECT
    -> ARCHITECTURE

  PROJECT_MODEL
    -> CHAINS

  CHAINS
    -> SKILLS

  PROMPTS
    -> TASKS

  TASKS
    -> STATE

  DECISIONS
    -> ARCHITECTURE
    -> PROJECT_MODEL
    -> CHAINS


Allowed:

  PROJECT:
    may_reference:
      PROJECT_MODEL
      ARCHITECTURE
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      PROMPTS
      TASKS
      DECISIONS
      STATE

  PROJECT_MODEL:
    may_reference:
      ARCHITECTURE
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      TASKS
      DECISIONS

  CHAINS:
    may_reference:
      SKILLS
      TASKS
      STATE
      DECISIONS

  TASKS:
    may_reference:
      PROJECT_MODEL
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      DECISIONS
      STATE

  PROMPTS:
    may_reference:
      PROJECT_MODEL
      ARCHITECTURE
      CHAINS
      SKILLS
      TASKS
      DECISIONS


Forbidden:

  SKILLS:
    MUST NOT redefine PROJECT architecture.

  TASKS:
    MUST NOT redefine global SDD architecture.

  PROMPTS:
    MUST NOT silently modify architecture or global rules.

  STATE:
    MUST NOT define architecture.

  PROJECT_MODEL:
    MUST NOT contain engineering skill instructions.

  ARCHITECTURE:
    MUST NOT contain implementation code.


Principles:

  SeparationOfConcerns:
    Each SDD component MUST own one responsibility.

  SingleSourceOfTruth:
    A fact MUST have one authoritative SDD owner.

  ExplicitReference:
    Cross-component knowledge MUST use references.

  Locality:
    Detailed knowledge MUST live as close as possible to
    the component that owns it.

  NoDuplication:
    The same knowledge MUST NOT be copied across multiple SDD files.

  Traceability:
    Every task MUST be traceable to a project element,
    chain stage or decision.

  HumanControl:
    Global architecture changes require human approval — this is the
    same rule as PROJECT.sdd -> Rules [R9]/[R11], not a separate
    definition; this file does not restate it as new authority, it just
    applies it to the SDD component graph above.

State: +
  (STEP 3 completed — see PROJECT.sdd -> Rules [R10]: state must be marked
  when a stage finishes.)

Note:
  (prompt/new/20.md) This file describes how .sdd's OWN components
  (PROJECT, PROJECT_MODEL, ARCHITECTURE, CHAINS, SKILLS, PROMPTS, TASKS,
  DECISIONS, STATE) relate to each other — it operates at the "meta" level.

  Important distinction (the names collide): `project/architecture.sdd` is
  the PROJECT's own technical architecture (stack, ModuleIsolation,
  LayerArchitecture — how FE/BE/DB call each other) and is COMPLETELY
  separate from this file. This file never duplicates that content (see
  NoDuplication above) — it only references PROJECT_MODEL as a component.

  (prompt/new/21.md, STEP 4 — RESOLVED) The stale `protocol/symbols.sdd`
  and `protocol/rules.sdd` references that `project/architecture.sdd` used
  to carry were removed by STEP 4's Full Reset; that file is now
  schema-only and carries no remaining reference to the retired
  `protocol/` tree (see .sdd/project/architecture.sdd -> Note,
  .sdd/PROJECT.sdd -> Note).

  (prompt/new/22.md, STEP 5 — RESOLVED) The CHAINS component's earlier
  "feature-per-chain" role description had gone stale — STEP 5's Full
  Reset removed `chains/_TEMPLATE.feature.sdd` and replaced it with the
  chain-type model (router + feature/bugfix/change.chain; see
  .sdd/chains/chains.sdd -> Note).

  (prompt/new/23.md, STEP 6) The SKILLS component's `role:` field carried a
  stale "STEP 9" marker left over from an earlier numbering scheme — wrong,
  since `.sdd/skills/` is actually built at this step (prompt/new/23.md),
  not the ninth. Corrected to "STEP 6". The CHAINS -> SKILLS relationship
  (see Relationships above) is now backed by a real file, `skills/
  skills.sdd` (see its Purpose section for the full four-layer map of
  project/architecture/chains/skills).

  (prompt/new/24.md, STEP 7 — RESOLVED) Added a "STEP 7" marker to the
  PROMPTS component's `role:` field, matching the convention used by the
  other components. The Forbidden-section rule "PROMPTS: MUST NOT silently
  modify architecture or global rules" is unchanged — the STEP 7 reset of
  `.sdd/prompts/` (see .sdd/prompts/prompts.sdd -> Note) only built
  PROMPTS' own internal structure; it did not touch this rule.

  (prompt/new/25.md, STEP 8 — RESOLVED, later reverted) The TASKS
  component's `role:` field previously described the "active/planned/
  completed" model, referencing the `.sdd/tasks/` layout of that time (6
  directories: active/backlog/blocked/review/done/failed). That structure
  no longer exists (see the STEP 8 correction note below) — kept here only
  as a historical record.

  (prompt/new/26.md, STEP 8 correction — RESOLVED) The TASKS component's
  `path:` field changed from `../tasks` to `../project/tasks.sdd`; `role:`
  was updated: TASKS was no longer a root-level directory but the
  `project/tasks.sdd` TaskModel router — concrete tasks lived under their
  owning domain via the `project/<domain>/tasks/*.sdd` convention (see the
  Locality/NoDuplication principles above, .sdd/project/tasks.sdd ->
  Storage, [T2]). The root-level `.sdd/tasks/` (README.md, tasks.sdd, 6
  directories) was fully deleted with user confirmation (Full Reset;
  preserved in git history).

  The TASKS entries in Relationships/Allowed/Forbidden (`PROMPTS -> TASKS`,
  `TASKS -> STATE`, `TASKS: may_reference: ...`, `TASKS: MUST NOT redefine
  global SDD architecture`) did NOT change — this step only moved where
  TASKS lives (path), not its role in the .sdd component graph.

  (prompt/new/27.md, STEP 8 final — RESOLVED) The STEP 8 correction (26.md)
  was itself corrected in turn — the source clarified that HOW a task
  works (lifecycle/creation/decomposition/dependency/state/review/failure/
  recovery/relocation/completion) is .sdd's own system behavior, not
  project knowledge. This is actually MORE portable staying at the root
  (`.sdd` carries this file unchanged into any new project it moves to)
  than it would be inside `project/`. The TASKS component's `path:` field
  changed from `../project/tasks.sdd` back to `../tasks/tasks.sdd`;
  `role:` was updated: TASKS is root-level again, but unlike the STEP 8
  (25.md) version it has no storage directories — it is ENGINE only (a
  rules file). Concrete task instances still live under their owning
  domain via `project/<domain>/tasks/*.sdd` (unchanged). `.sdd/project/
  tasks.sdd` (created by 26.md) was fully deleted (preserved in git
  history) — see .sdd/tasks/tasks.sdd -> Note. Relationships/Allowed/
  Forbidden again did NOT change.

  The source template (prompt/new/20.md) was kept almost as-is, since its
  Components/Relationships/Allowed/Forbidden/Principles structure was
  already clear and complete; the only added best-practice pieces are this
  Note, HumanControl's explicit reference to R9/R11, and the `State:` line
  (per R10).

  (prompt/new/28.md, STEP 8 refinement — additive, Full Reset NOT
  required) The TASKS component's `path:`/`role:` fields did NOT change
  (still `../tasks/tasks.sdd`, still the ENGINE-vs-MODEL explanation) —
  this step only enriched `tasks.sdd`'s own internal structure (see
  ../tasks/tasks.sdd -> Note); it had no effect on the .sdd component
  graph (Relationships/Allowed/Forbidden).

  (prompt/new/29.md, STEP 9 — additive, Full Reset NOT required) The
  DECISIONS component's `path:` (already `../decisions`) and its entries
  in Relationships/Allowed/Forbidden were already declared before this
